    \;

    \;

    \;

    \;

    <\padded-center>
      <section|The Very Beginning: 1950 \U 1960>
    </padded-center>
  </hidden>|<\hidden>
    David H. Hubel and Torsten Wiesel published a paper in <em|The Journal of
    Physiology> entitled: `Receptive fields of single neurons in the cat's
    striate cortex', 1959.

    <image|image/cat_visual_cortext.png|.42par|||><space|1em><image|image/Hubel_Weisel.png|.5par|||>

    <\with|font-base-size|8>
      Hubel and Wiesel's experiment.<space|9em>Hubel and Weisel after winning
      their Nobel Prize, 1981.\ 

      https://sharedhistories.ca/nobelcanadian/david-hubel/the-scientist/
    </with>
  </hidden>|<\hidden>
    Frank Rosenblatt introduced the Perceptron in 1957, an early neural
    network model that could learn to recognize simple patterns.

    <\padded-center>
      <image|image/Frank_Rosenblatt_perceptron.png|.3par|||>
    </padded-center>

    <with|font-base-size|8|Frank Rosenblatt at age 32, 1960, wiring the Mark
    1 Perceptron.(https://perceptrondemo.com/)>
  </hidden>|<\hidden>
    <\padded-center>
      <image|image/Frank_Rosenblatt_image_recognition.png|.5par|||>
    </padded-center>

    <with|font-base-size|8|Frank Rosenblatt working on automated image
    recognition, attaching a camera system to his Mark 1 Perceptron at
    Cornell University. (https://perceptrondemo.com/)>
  </hidden>|<\hidden>
    Optical character recognition also took its first steps during this era.

    <\padded-center>
      <image|image/ocr.png|.7par|||>

      <with|font-base-size|8|https://www.docsumo.com/blog/optical-character-recognition-history>
    </padded-center>
  </hidden>|<\hidden>
    \;

    \;

    \;

    \;

    <\padded-center>
      <section|The Birth of a Discipline (1960 \U 1970)>
    </padded-center>
  </hidden>|<\hidden>
    The 1960s marked the formal beginning of computer vision as a research
    discipline.

    Lawrence G. Roberts(Larry Roberts), the father of computer vision,
    published his 1963 MIT dissertation on machine perception of
    three-dimensional solids.

    <space|2em><image|image/Larry_Roberts_block.png|.25par|||><space|5em><image|image/Larry_Roberts.png|.5par|||>

    <with|font-base-size|6|https://epicsysinc.com/blog/machine-vision-history-3/<space|2em>https://computerhistory.org/blog/2017-chm-fellow-lawrence-g-roberts/>
  </hidden>|<\hidden>
    <space|3em><image|image/1966summer.png|.8par|||>
  </hidden>|<\hidden>
    \;

    \;

    \;

    \;

    <\padded-center>
      <section|Pattern Recognition/Image Processing(1970\U 1980)>
    </padded-center>
  </hidden>|<\hidden>
    David Marr at MIT proposed a powerful computational theory of vision in
    the late 1970s that divided visual processing into layers: primal
    sketch,two-and-a-half-D sketch,and full 3D model.

    <with|color|blue|<hlink||https://direct.mit.edu/books/monograph/3299/VisionA-Computational-Investigation-into-the-Human>Vision:
    A Computational Investigation into the Human Representation and
    Processing of Visual Information>

    <image|image/David_Marr.png|.2par|||><space|4em><image|image/stages_of_visual_representation_marr.png|0.55par|||>

    <with|font-base-size|5|http://buttondown.com/bjbalas/archive/science-of-chess-david-marr-candidate
    http://museonghwang.github.io/cs231n/2022/07/03/cs231n-lecture1>
  </hidden>|<\hidden>
    Edge detection became a major focus during this decade. Researchers
    developed techniques to find boundaries between objects in an image.\ 

    <space|2em><image|image/edge.png|.65par|||>

    <with|font-base-size|8|https://in.mathworks.com/matlabcentral/fileexchange/40737-canny-edge-detector>
  </hidden>|<\hidden>
    \;

    <\padded-center>
      <image|image/edge1.png|1par|||>
    </padded-center>

    <with|font-base-size|8|https://www.datacamp.com/tutorial/feature-extraction-machine-learning>
  </hidden>|<\hidden>
    The use of statistical models to classify objects and signals.

    \;

    <space|3em><image|image/feature_extraction_classification.png|.7par|||>

    <with|font-base-size|7|https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/Shoeprint-Image-Database-Image-Pre-Processing-Label-Rida-Bakshi/9b0bd1f813e4861de1814800011cc7b292a010c4/figure/2>
  </hidden>|<\hidden>
    Hidden Markov models, originally developed for speech, began to find
    applications in visual sequence analysis as well.

    <space|4em><image|image/hmm.png|.7par|||>

    <with|font-base-size|8|https://pjvenezuela.org/hidden-markov-model-diagram-hidden-markov-model-python/>
  </hidden>|<\hidden>
    \;

    \;

    \;

    \;

    <\padded-center>
      <section|Neural Networks and Early Learning (1980 \U 1990)>
    </padded-center>
  </hidden>|<\hidden>
    In 1980, \<#798F\>\<#5C9B\>\<#90A6\>\<#5F66\>(Kunihiko Fukushima)
    published the neocognitron, the original deep convolutional neural
    network (CNN) architecture

    <space|5em><image|image/neocognitron.png|.6par|||>

    <with|font-base-size|8|https://labelyourdata.com/articles/history-of-machine-learning-how-did-it-all-start>
  </hidden>|<\hidden>
    <\padded-center>
      <image|image/history_bp.png|.7par|||>

      <with|font|AR PL New Sung|font-family|sans|font-base-size|5|https://techhistorylab.com/history-of-backpropagation-deep-learning/>
    </padded-center>
  </hidden>|<\hidden>
    <space|4em><image|image/who_bp.png|.7par|||>

    <space|11em><with|font|AR PL New Sung|font-family|sans|font-base-size|5|https://techhistorylab.com/who-invented-backpropagation-ai/>
  </hidden>|<\hidden>
    Yann LeCun advanced this work throughout the decade and into the early
    1990s by developing LeNet, a convolutional neural network that could
    reliably read handwritten digits.

    <space|9em><image|image/yanlecun_cnn.png|.45par|||>\ 
  </hidden>|<\hidden>
    Sample images from MNIST dataset

    <space|4em><image|image/mnist.png|.7par|||>

    <with|font-base-size|8|https://handwiki.org/wiki/LeNet>
  </hidden>|<\hidden>
    \;

    \;

    <image|image/lenet5.png|1par|||>

    <with|font-base-size|8|https://www.educative.io/blog/lenet-5>
  </hidden>|<\hidden>
    Image segmentation also matured during the 1980s.

    <\padded-center>
      <image|image/image_segmentation.png|.6par|||>

      <with|font-base-size|8|https://techhistorylab.com/history-of-image-segmentation/>
    </padded-center>
  </hidden>|<\hidden>
    \;

    \;

    \;

    \;

    <\padded-center>
      <section|<section*|The Rise of Computer Vision Tools (1990 \U 2006)>>
    </padded-center>
  </hidden>|<\hidden>
    <space|4em><image|image/opencv.png|.7par|||>

    <with|font-base-size|8|https://techhistorylab.com/history-of-opencv/>
  </hidden>|<\hidden>
    <space|5em><image|image/python.png|.7par|||>

    <with|font-base-size|8|https://techhistorylab.com/history-of-python-1989-to-today/>
  </hidden>|<\hidden>
    <space|3em><image|image/nvidia.png|.7par|||>

    <with|font-base-size|8|https://techhistorylab.com/history-of-gpu-in-ai-nvidia-deep-learning/>
  </hidden>|<\hidden>
    Turk and Pentland published their Eigenfaces approach in 1991, using
    principal component analysis to represent and compare faces
    mathematically.

    <space|4em><image|image/eigenface.png|.7par|||>

    <with|font-base-size|8|https://www.baeldung.com/cs/cv-eigenfaces>
  </hidden>|<\hidden>
    <space|4em><image|image/history_viola_jones.png|.7par|||>

    <with|font-base-size|8|https://techhistorylab.com/viola-jones-algorithm/>
  </hidden>|<\hidden>
    \;

    \;

    \;

    \;

